.5 resigned after 76 turns. The game was close early on, with Black ahead by +4.0 after move 8 and roughly level through the middlegame. Then a run of costly Black moves (F5, B3, E8, F8) pushed the lead past -25, and J3 at move 31 lost 24.5 points. From there Opus 5.5 never got back into the game.", "moments": [{"turn": 8, "text": "White's H3 lost 3.9 points, and the engine preferred H4. It still left Black ahead at +4.0, the best Opus 5.5 looked all game. Black's next few moves gave that lead back."}, {"turn": 19, "text": "Black's F5 dropped from +0.6 to -7.1, a 7.7-point loss against the engine's B5. This was the first big swing, and it came just after White had stretched a long row along the fourth line."}, {"turn": 27, "text": "Black's F8 lost 12.0 points (the engine wanted B2) and took the lead from -13.5 to -25.5. Black was pushing in the upper middle while the lower left was still open."}, {"turn": 28, "text": "White's E6 lost 15.8 points, the 14k's worst move, and the engine preferred A2. It brought the lead back from -25.5 to -9.7, so Black's chance to get back into the game was real and short."}, {"turn": 31, "text": "Black's J3 lost 24.5 points, the worst move of the game, and the engine's choice was B2. The lead fell from -9.1 to -33.6, and the rest of the game barely moved it."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py, libs.py and count.py each work only on a pasted position: after.py applies one player-named move, removes captures and prints group liberties; libs.py lists group liberties; count.py counts stones and enclosed territory. None loops over empty points to score candidates or plays any continuation forward, so the player still chose every move itself.", "stop": false}}